{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And bless himself in his heart, saying</b>: When the others hear the curses being invoked with these imprecations, he will bless himself in his thought and say in his heart, 'Peace will be mine despite all these, as I walk in the desire of my heart.' And HaShem will not forgive him, but His anger will smoke against him then, when he sins in his generation or in generations to come. Alternatively, the force of כי (ki) may be taken as: 'I will have peace, because I am not accepting the oath upon myself, for in the stubbornness of my heart I will walk all my days, and I will do whatever I desire.' And YHWH will not be willing, because he does not accept His imprecation, to forgive him, but His anger will smoke against him, since he entered into His covenant before Him with all Israel. A desire that overpowers and grows strong in the heart is called שרירות (sharirut, stubbornness), as in the language of the Sages (Bava Batra 160a): שריר וקיים (sharir ve-kayam, 'confirmed and established'), and likewise בשרירי בטנו (be-shrirai vitno, 'the sinews of his belly,' Job 40:16)." "And the meaning of <b>so that the watered sweeps along the thirsty</b> is: to add the sated one to the desiring one. For a sated soul is called רוה (revah, watered), as in the instances 'and I filled the soul of the priests with fatness' and 'my people shall be satisfied with my goodness' (Jeremiah 31:13), and 'their soul shall be like a watered garden' (ibid. v. 11); and the desiring soul is called צמאה (tsemayah, thirsty), as in 'my soul thirsts for You' (Psalms 63:2). The explanation is that when the soul of a person is sated and does not desire evil things for itself, if some desire enters his heart and he fulfills that desire, he then adds to his soul an even greater desire, and he becomes very thirsty for that same thing which he ate or did, more than at first, and he will also desire other evil things that he had not desired initially. For one who lusts after beautiful women — once he is swept away in that lust, a desire will come upon him to lie with a male or with an animal, and so too with all other desires. This is as the Sages said (Sukkah 52b): 'One who satiates the hungry, starves the sated.' Therefore Scripture says concerning one who walks in the stubbornness of his heart — that is, if he fills his soul with strong and overpowering desires to which it is thirsty — his sated soul will be swept along with the thirsty one, for he will desire and thirst for that from which he had already been sated, and for that with which he had sated his soul. And because Scripture mentioned <b>a root bearing</b>, it used this imagery, for the root — that is, his gall — which is watered and fresh, will be swept along with the thirsty, and all of them will turn thirsty. And <b>למען</b> (lema'an, 'so that') in this place does not carry the sense of 'I will walk in the stubbornness of my heart in order that I sweep along the watered with the thirsty,' but it is like <b>לספות</b> (lisfot, 'to add'): Scripture says that because of this it will come to pass thus — for in every cause combined with an effect, Scripture says <b>למען</b> (lema'an), whether a person performs the cause that produces the effect intentionally, as in 'so that it may go well with you,' or whether a person brings it about unintentionally, as in 'so that the heart may melt' (Ezekiel 21:20), 'so that they may be guilty' (ibid. 6:6), 'their silver and their gold they made into idols for themselves, so that they may be cut off' (Hosea 8:4), and many like these. This is what is good and right in my eyes for this verse, above all that has been written on it in many ways. It is also possible to interpret that Scripture returns to the subject of 'a root bearing gall and wormwood,' saying that when one blesses himself in his heart saying 'peace will be mine,' it is so that his flourishing growth — the watered — which grows and sprouts the gall and wormwood, will be added upon the thirsty that had not been growing. Or <b>הרוה</b> (ha-reveh, 'the watered') may be an appellation for the wormwood mentioned, that the wormwood, which is watered and sprouts and grows greatly, will be added upon the thirsty that had not been growing except a little, and both of them will be growing. The intent of this is to say that anyone who hears the imprecation and blesses himself from it and strengthens himself in his stubbornness — his branches will bring him back to sin and will sprout transgressions more than at first. This too is a correct interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:57.999255754Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}